<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kholodny Yar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kholodny Yar is a forested area of ravines near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chyhyryn">Chyhyryn</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/motronynskyi-monastery">Motronynskyi Monastery</a>. It is linked in Ukrainian memory with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a> and the haidamaka rising of 1768. From 1919 to 1922 local partisans there held a self-governing district against both White and Bolshevik forces, later celebrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-horlis-horsky">Yuri Horlis-Horsky</a>'s memoir of the same name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kholodny-yar.jpg" alt="Kholodny Yar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uk-Kamelot · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9C%D0%BE%D1%82%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BD%D1%81%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%801.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kholodnyi Yar, whose name means Cold Ravine, is a relict forest of 6,804 hectares in Cherkasy Raion of Cherkasy Oblast. Forest covers 6,509 hectares, 95.7 per cent of the tract, most of it planted, and oak makes up 81 per cent of the woodland. About 150 species of herbaceous plants grow there, some of them listed in the Red Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. The forest holds more than 150 archaeological, historical and scientific sites, among them mounds of the Scythian period and later, and archaeologists have found traces of every archaeological culture of the area from the Trypillian culture onwards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first written mention of Kholodnyi Yar comes from historians describing the battles of Lithuanian and Rus' soldiers against the Horde at the Blue Water in 1363–1367. Because of the nearness of the Wild Fields and the raids of nomadic peoples, the lands long had no settled population, and for centuries a few fortress towns and fortified monasteries were the only settlements between Kaniv, Cherkasy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> rapids.</p><p>In the 1730s the Kholodnyi Yar Sich was organised in the forest, which for decades was the main base of the haidamaks. From 1919 to 1922 it was the centre of the Kholodny Yar Republic, a self-proclaimed state and partisan movement based in the forest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1968 the Council of Ministers of the Ukrainian SSR declared 553 hectares a nature monument of national significance, and 561 hectares in all belong to the nature reserve fund. On 1 January 2022 the Kholodnyi Yar National Nature Park was created on the territory of the forest.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kholodnyi Yar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Kholodny Yar</b> is a forested ravine area near Chyhyryn, rich in memories of the Cossack Hetmanate and the 18th-century haidamaka rebels.</blockquote><a href="/state/kholodny-yar-republic">Kholodny Yar Republic</a></li></ul></section>
